{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I've been reviewing why you came in tonight. You were having very high blood sugar, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it was over 600 earlier. I took my Lantus and Humalog, but it’s not coming down like it should."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You also mentioned fevers up to 104 and mouth ulcers this week. Are those still bothering you?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:19:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The fever broke yesterday, but my mouth still hurts. I can’t eat much. I’m on acyclovir for the sores."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. The high sugars and ketones in your urine mean you’re in a state called diabetic ketoacidosis, even though your blood work doesn’t show an acid buildup. We’ll keep an eye on that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:22:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I feel okay now. Why did I need to come back so soon after my last hospital stay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Even if you feel stable, very high sugars with ketones can be dangerous. We want to get your levels under control safely before sending you home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:25:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. My stomach pain is back too—same as always, in the middle, going to my back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:27:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your exam shows epigastric tenderness. But your lipase is normal, so it’s likely your chronic pain from gastroparesis or pancreatitis, not a new flare-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I haven’t had a bowel movement in two days. Is that part of it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2157-02-27 22:30:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, gastroparesis and pain meds can slow things down. We’ll monitor that. For now, we’re continuing your home insulin—90 units Lantus and sliding scale Humalog."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2157-02-28 07:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood sugar is improving—it’s in the 200s now. We ran tests for flu and a urine culture. Both came back negative, so no infection we can find."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2157-02-28 07:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So the fevers were just from the virus? Not something serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2157-02-28 07:33:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Looks that way. No sign of bacterial infection, white count is normal, and you’re not spiking fevers now. We think it was a viral illness that triggered the high sugars."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2157-02-28 07:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I eat something? I’m really hungry, but I’m scared it’ll make my stomach hurt worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2157-02-28 07:36:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re switching you to oral Dilaudid and you’ve tolerated sips. You can try a light breakfast. Start small and let us know how you feel."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well today—eating, pain controlled, sugars stable. We’re planning to discharge you this afternoon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I get a new prescription for pain meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Per your narcotics agreement, your primary care provider will manage all pain prescriptions, even after discharge. We won’t be writing any here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I understand. And my insulin? Should I keep doing the same thing?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue your 90 units Lantus at night and Humalog as needed. Watch for high sugars, especially if you’re sick again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my mouth sores? They make it hard to eat."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:09:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep taking acyclovir as prescribed. Stick to soft, bland foods and stay hydrated. If they don’t improve, follow up with your doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my stomach pain—should I go to the ER every time it flares?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:12:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not necessarily. If your lipase stays normal and you’re not vomiting or spiking fevers, it’s likely your chronic condition. But always check your sugars and ketones."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:14:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll discharge you in a couple of hours. A nurse will go over instructions and your follow-up plan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel better knowing it’s not something worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2157-02-28 12:17:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and don’t hesitate to reach out if things change."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2157-02-27 22:07:00",
    "admission_end": "2157-02-28 16:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted for uncontrolled type I diabetes with hyperglycemia and ketonuria, consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis, following a viral illness with high fevers and oral herpes. She had no anion gap and stable vital signs, with normal lipase ruling out acute pancreatitis. Her blood glucose improved with continuation of home insulin therapy. Fevers resolved without identified bacterial source, and flu testing was negative. Chronic abdominal pain was managed with transition to oral Dilaudid, and she tolerated oral intake. Per narcotics agreement, no hospital prescriptions were issued. She was discharged with stable glucose levels, instructions to continue home insulin, and follow-up with her primary care provider for pain and oral lesions.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with ketoacidosis, type I [juvenile type], uncontrolled",
      "Fever, unspecified",
      "Other chronic pain",
      "Abdominal pain, unspecified site",
      "Anxiety state, unspecified",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Borderline personality disorder",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
